{"name":"Tuscan Bread","hex":"#e7d2ad","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bread","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A foodstuff made by baking dough made from cereals.","A foodstuff made by baking dough made from cereals."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To coat with breadcrumbs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bread"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pane sciocco","blocks":[{"text":"Pane sciocco or pane toscano outside Tuscany, is a type of bread commonly found in Tuscany, Umbria, and Marche regions of Italy, distinguished from other Italian breads by a lack of salt and slightly acidic crumb. Pane sciocco is often eaten with Tuscan condiments such as pecorino toscano cheese, ham, sausages, and prosciutto."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Pane_toscano.jpg/330px-Pane_toscano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pane_sciocco"},{"title":"Bread","blocks":[{"text":"Bread is a baked food product made from water, flour, and often yeast. It is a staple food across the world, particularly in Europe and the Middle East. Throughout recorded history and around the world, it has been an important part of many cultures' diets. It is one of the oldest human-made foods, having been of significance since the dawn of agriculture, and plays an essential role in both religious rituals and secular culture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Korb_mit_Br%C3%B6tchen.JPG/330px-Korb_mit_Br%C3%B6tchen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bread"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Woman_baking_bread-E_25213-IMG_4514-gradient.jpg/500px-Woman_baking_bread-E_25213-IMG_4514-gradient.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm